## p3-se | The Schrödinger equation | physics | 2 | phys3 | p3-oldq
explain 3 | The Schrödinger equation
- $ iħ ∂ψ/∂t = −(ħ²/2m) ∂²ψ/∂x² + Vψ
- ψ is a complex wavefunction. |ψ|² is a probability density (Born, 1926).
- It's deterministic: ψ evolves smoothly. Randomness only enters at measurement.
? What does |ψ(x)|² represent? :: The probability density of finding the particle at x
> Stationary states have definite energy

explain 3 | Stationary states
- If ψ = φ(x)e^(−iEt/ħ), the time-independent equation is Hφ = Eφ.
- |ψ|² doesn't change in time: nothing observable moves.
- *Atoms in stationary states don't radiate.* That fixes Bohr's problem.
> Normalisation: probabilities add to 1

quiz 3 | ψ(x) = A for 0 < x < 2 and zero elsewhere. What must A be?
* 1/√2
o 1/2
o 2
o √2
= ∫|ψ|² dx = 2A² = 1.
> Expectation values are averages over many measurements

explain 4 | Expectation values
- ⟨x⟩ = ∫ψ* x ψ dx. ⟨p⟩ = ∫ψ*(−iħ ∂/∂x)ψ dx.
- They're the average results of measuring many identically prepared systems.
- *Ehrenfest's theorem: expectation values obey Newton's laws* (approximately).
> Next: solvable potentials

## p3-1d | 1D quantum potentials | physics | 2 | phys3 | p3-se
quiz 3 | Electron in a 1 nm infinite well. If the ground state energy is E₁, what is E₃?
* 9E₁
o 3E₁
o 6E₁
o 27E₁
= Eₙ ∝ n².
> The quantum harmonic oscillator has equally spaced levels

explain 4 | The quantum harmonic oscillator
- $ Eₙ = ħω(n + ½)
- Equally spaced, and the lowest energy is ½ħω, not zero: *zero-point energy*.
- Molecular vibrations, phonons and the electromagnetic field are all harmonic oscillators.
? Energy levels of the quantum harmonic oscillator? :: Eₙ = ħω(n + ½)
> Ladder operators climb the levels

explain 5 | Ladder operators
- Define a and a† from x and p. Then H = ħω(a†a + ½).
- a† raises the energy by ħω; a lowers it. a|0⟩ = 0.
- *In quantum field theory, a† creates a particle.* The same algebra, promoted.
> Tunnelling depends exponentially on barrier width

quiz 4 | Doubling a barrier's width changes the tunnelling probability roughly how?
* Squares it (e.g. 10⁻³ → 10⁻⁶)
o Halves it
o Doubles it
o No change
= Transmission ≈ e^(−2κL). Double L: square the factor.
= That exponential sensitivity is what scanning tunnelling microscopes exploit.
> STMs image individual atoms this way

real 5 | The scanning tunnelling microscope
- A sharp tip hovers a few atoms above a surface. Electrons tunnel across the gap.
- The current changes about 10× for every 0.1 nm, so it maps atomic bumps.
- *1990: IBM spelled out "IBM" with 35 xenon atoms using one.*
> Next: the hydrogen atom

## p3-h | The hydrogen atom | physics | 3 | phys3 | p3-1d
explain 4 | Four quantum numbers label every electron state
- n (energy shell), l = 0…n−1 (orbital angular momentum), m_l = −l…l, m_s = ±½.
- l = 0, 1, 2, 3 are called s, p, d, f.
- *The periodic table's shape is these rules plus Pauli exclusion.*
? Allowed values of l for a given n? :: 0, 1, …, n − 1
> Count the states in a shell

quiz 3 | How many electron states (including spin) in the n = 3 shell?
* 18
o 9
o 6
o 32
= 2n² = 18: one 3s, three 3p, five 3d orbitals, two spins each.
> Spectral lines are fingerprints

real 4 | Helium was discovered in the Sun before Earth
- 1868: a yellow spectral line in sunlight matched no known element.
- It was named after Helios. Helium wasn't isolated on Earth until 1895.
- *Spectroscopy tells us what stars are made of without visiting.*
> Fine structure: relativity tweaks the levels

explain 5 | Fine structure and the 21 cm line
- Relativistic and spin-orbit corrections split levels by about α² ≈ 1/18,800 of the gross energies.
- α ≈ 1/137 is the fine-structure constant.
- Hyperfine splitting of hydrogen's ground state emits the 21 cm radio line that maps our galaxy's gas.
> Next: spin

## p3-spin | Spin and angular momentum | physics | 3 | phys3 | p3-h
story 5 | Stern–Gerlach: silver atoms split in two
- 1922: a beam of silver atoms through a non-uniform magnetic field split into two spots, not a smear.
- Angular momentum along any axis comes in discrete values.
- *Stern and Gerlach didn't know it, but they'd seen electron spin.*
> Spin-½ measured along different axes

quiz 4 | A spin-½ particle is measured "up" along z, then measured along x. Probability of "up" along x?
* 50%
o 100%
o 0%
o 25%
= |⟨+x|+z⟩|² = 1/2.
= Measuring along z again afterwards gives 50/50 too: the x measurement erased the z information.
> Angular momentum components don't commute

explain 4 | You can't know Lx and Lz together
- [Lx, Ly] = iħLz and cyclic.
- So you can only know L² and one component simultaneously.
- *That's why we label states by l and m_l but not by three components.*
> Spin determines statistics

## p3-ident | Identical particles | physics | 3 | phys3 | p3-spin
explain 4 | Fermions and bosons
- Swap two identical particles: the wavefunction picks up +1 (bosons) or −1 (fermions).
- Half-integer spin: fermions (electrons, quarks). Integer spin: bosons (photons, gluons).
- *Spin-statistics theorem: this link follows from relativity plus quantum mechanics.*
> Fermions avoid each other, bosons pile up

real 4 | Lasers are bosons piling into one state
- Photons are bosons: stimulated emission makes more photons copying an existing one.
- Billions end up in the same mode: same frequency, phase and direction.
- *Laser = Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation.* Einstein predicted the process in 1917.
> Neutron stars are held up by fermions refusing to share

explain 4 | Degeneracy pressure holds up dead stars
- White dwarfs are supported by electrons that can't be squeezed into the same states.
- Above about 1.4 solar masses (Chandrasekhar's limit), electrons lose and the star collapses further.
- *Neutron stars are held up mainly by neutron degeneracy pressure* plus nuclear forces.
? What is the Chandrasekhar limit? :: About 1.4 solar masses, the maximum mass of a white dwarf
> Next: the nucleus

## p3-nuc | Nuclear physics | physics | 2 | phys3 | p3-ident
explain 3 | Radioactive decay
- Alpha: emits a helium nucleus. Beta: a neutron turns into a proton, emitting an electron and antineutrino. Gamma: a high-energy photon.
- Each nucleus decays at random. Large numbers follow N = N₀e^(−λt).
- Half-life t½ = ln 2/λ.
> Iron is the bottom of the energy valley

explain 4 | The binding energy curve
- Binding energy per nucleon peaks around iron and nickel.
- Fusing light nuclei and splitting heavy ones both release energy.
- *Stars build elements up to iron. Heavier ones come mostly from supernovae and neutron star mergers.*
> Gold was forged in colliding neutron stars

real 5 | Much of the universe's gold came from neutron star collisions
- 2017: gravitational waves from merging neutron stars (GW170817) were seen along with light from the explosion.
- The light showed signs of freshly made heavy elements.
- *A substantial share of the gold in your jewellery was likely forged this way.*
> Fission was discovered in 1938

story 4 | Lise Meitner explained fission on a winter walk
- 1938: Hahn and Strassmann found barium after bombarding uranium with neutrons.
- Meitner and Frisch, walking in the snow in Sweden, worked out that the nucleus had split, using E = mc².
- *Hahn got the 1944 Nobel Prize alone.* Meitner's role is now widely recognised.
> Chain reactions need critical mass

## p3-part | Particle physics | physics | 3 | phys3 | p3-nuc
explain 3 | The Standard Model in one card
- Matter: 6 quarks and 6 leptons (electron, muon, tau and three neutrinos).
- Forces: photon (EM), W and Z (weak), 8 gluons (strong).
- The Higgs boson gives mass to the W, Z and fermions.
- *Gravity isn't in it.*
> Quarks are never seen alone

explain 4 | Confinement: why you can't pull quarks apart
- The strong force between quarks doesn't weaken with distance like gravity or EM does.
- Pull two apart and the energy in the gluon field makes new quark–antiquark pairs.
- *You get more hadrons, never a free quark.*
> The Higgs was found in 2012

story 4 | 48 years from prediction to discovery
- 1964: Higgs and others predicted a field that gives particles mass, and a boson to go with it.
- 2012: CERN's ATLAS and CMS experiments found it at about 125 GeV.
- *Higgs and Englert shared the 2013 Nobel Prize.*
> Neutrinos pass through you by the trillion

quiz 3 | About how many solar neutrinos pass through your thumbnail each second?
* About 60 billion
o About 1,000
o About 1 million
o None reach Earth
= Roughly 6 × 10¹⁰ per cm² per second. Almost all pass straight through Earth too.
> Neutrinos change flavour as they travel

story 4 | The solar neutrino problem
- From the 1960s, Ray Davis's detector found only about a third of the expected solar neutrinos.
- The answer, confirmed around 2001: neutrinos oscillate between types, so detectors that see one type miss the others.
- *Oscillation means neutrinos have mass,* which the original Standard Model didn't allow.
> Next: statistical mechanics

## p3-stat | Statistical mechanics | physics | 3 | phys3 | p3-part
explain 4 | The Boltzmann factor
- At temperature T, a state with energy E is occupied with probability ∝ e^(−E/k_BT).
- k_BT at room temperature ≈ 1/40 eV.
- *That's why chemistry happens at room temperature but nuclear reactions don't.*
? k_BT at room temperature, in eV? :: About 1/40 eV (0.025 eV)
> Equipartition: ½k_BT per quadratic degree of freedom

quiz 4 | Equipartition: average energy of a diatomic gas molecule at room temperature?
* (5/2)k_BT
o (3/2)k_BT
o (7/2)k_BT
o k_BT
= 3 translational + 2 rotational degrees of freedom. Vibrations are "frozen out" at room temperature.
= Classical physics couldn't explain the freezing. Quantum energy gaps do.
> Phase transitions and universality

explain 5 | Universality at critical points
- Near a critical point, a magnet and a liquid–gas system obey identical scaling laws.
- Microscopic details wash out: only dimension and symmetry matter.
- *Kenneth Wilson's renormalisation group explained this. Nobel Prize, 1982.*
> Next: the formal machinery of quantum mechanics

## p3-dirac | Dirac notation and operators | physics | 3 | phys3 | p3-stat
explain 4 | Bras, kets and inner products
- A state is a ket |ψ⟩: a vector in a Hilbert space.
- ⟨φ|ψ⟩ is the overlap. |⟨φ|ψ⟩|² is the probability of finding φ when in state ψ.
- *Change basis and the vector stays the same; only its components change.*
> Observables are Hermitian operators

explain 4 | Observables and eigenvalues
- Each measurable quantity is a Hermitian operator A.
- Possible outcomes are its eigenvalues (always real). After measuring, the state is the eigenvector.
- Probability of outcome aᵢ: |⟨aᵢ|ψ⟩|².
? What are the possible results of measuring observable A? :: The eigenvalues of the Hermitian operator A
> Time evolution is a unitary operator

quiz 4 | Two observables can be measured simultaneously with certainty when their operators…
* Commute
o Are both Hermitian
o Have the same eigenvalues
o Anticommute
= Commuting operators share a set of eigenvectors.
> Density matrices handle uncertainty about the state itself

explain 5 | Density matrices
- ρ = Σpᵢ|ψᵢ⟩⟨ψᵢ| describes a statistical mix of states.
- Pure state: Tr(ρ²) = 1. Mixed: less than 1.
- *Entangle a qubit with its environment and it looks mixed: decoherence.*
> Next: approximations when exact solutions fail

## p3-pert | Perturbation theory | physics | 4 | phys3 | p3-dirac
explain 4 | First-order energy shift
- H = H₀ + λV, with V small.
- $ Eₙ⁽¹⁾ = ⟨n|V|n⟩
- The first-order shift is just the average of the perturbation in the unperturbed state.
? First-order energy correction in perturbation theory? :: ⟨n|V|n⟩
> Second order pushes levels apart

explain 5 | Level repulsion
- Second order: Eₙ⁽²⁾ = Σ |⟨m|V|n⟩|²/(Eₙ − Eₘ) over m ≠ n.
- The ground state always goes down at second order.
- *Coupled levels repel each other:* the avoided crossings seen all over molecular physics.
> Fermi's golden rule gives transition rates

explain 5 | Fermi's golden rule
- $ Γ = (2π/ħ)|⟨f|V|i⟩|² ρ(E_f)
- Transition rate = (coupling squared) × (density of final states).
- *Radioactive decay rates, light absorption and scattering cross-sections all use it.*
> Next: scattering

## p3-scat | Scattering theory | physics | 4 | phys3 | p3-pert
story 4 | Rutherford found the nucleus by surprise
- 1909: Geiger and Marsden fired alpha particles at thin gold foil. A few bounced almost straight back.
- Rutherford: "as if you fired a 15-inch shell at a piece of tissue paper and it came back and hit you".
- *Atoms are almost entirely empty, with a tiny dense nucleus.*
> The cross-section measures effective target size

explain 4 | Cross-sections
- dσ/dΩ: how many particles scatter into a given direction, per unit incoming flux.
- Rutherford: dσ/dΩ ∝ 1/sin⁴(θ/2).
- Units of area: particle physicists use the barn, 10⁻²⁸ m² ("as big as a barn" for a nucleus).
> The Born approximation is a Fourier transform

explain 5 | The Born approximation
- For weak potentials, the scattering amplitude ∝ Fourier transform of V at momentum transfer q.
- Scattering experiments literally measure the Fourier transform of the target.
- *X-ray crystallography works the same way.* That's how DNA's double helix was read.
> Next: quantum fields

## p3-qft | Quantum field theory ideas | physics | 4 | phys3 | p3-scat
explain 5 | Particles are excitations of fields
- Every particle type is a ripple in its own field filling all of space.
- Every electron is identical because they're all excitations of one electron field.
- Creation and annihilation operators add and remove quanta.
> Feynman diagrams are bookkeeping

explain 4 | Feynman diagrams
- Lines are particles; vertices are interactions with a coupling strength.
- Each diagram is one term in a perturbation series.
- *QED predicts the electron's magnetic moment to over 10 significant figures.* One of the most precise matches in science.
> Virtual particles aren't little balls

myth 4 F | Virtual particles pop in and out of existence everywhere, as real particles briefly borrowing energy.
= Virtual particles are internal lines in a perturbation calculation, not observable things.
= The "borrowing energy" story is a popular metaphor, not what the maths says.
= Vacuum effects like the Casimir force and the Lamb shift are real, though.
> Renormalisation tamed the infinities

story 5 | Renormalisation: infinities that cancel
- Early QED calculations gave infinite answers.
- Feynman, Schwinger and Tomonaga showed the infinities can be absorbed into the measured mass and charge.
- *Nobel Prize 1965.* Feynman still called it "a dippy process".
> Next: gravity and the universe

## p3-gr | General relativity and cosmology | physics | 4 | phys3 | p3-qft
explain 4 | Einstein's field equations
- $ G_μν = (8πG/c⁴) T_μν
- Left: curvature of spacetime. Right: energy and momentum.
- *"Spacetime tells matter how to move; matter tells spacetime how to curve."* (John Wheeler)
> Light bends around the Sun

story 5 | 1919: the eclipse that made Einstein famous
- GR predicted starlight grazing the Sun bends by 1.75 arcseconds, twice the Newtonian value.
- Eddington's expedition to Príncipe measured it during a total eclipse.
- *Headlines worldwide: "Revolution in Science".*
> The universe is expanding

quiz 3 | Hubble's law: v = H₀d with H₀ ≈ 70 km/s per megaparsec. Recession speed of a galaxy 100 Mpc away?
* About 7,000 km/s
o About 70 km/s
o About 700,000 km/s
o About 0.7 km/s
= 70 × 100.
= 1/H₀ gives roughly the age of the universe: about 14 billion years.
> The cosmic microwave background is the Big Bang's afterglow

story 5 | Two engineers mistook the Big Bang for pigeon droppings
- 1964: Penzias and Wilson found a persistent microwave hiss from every direction.
- They cleaned pigeon droppings out of their antenna. The hiss stayed.
- *It was the 2.7 K afterglow of the early universe. Nobel Prize, 1978.*
> Most of the universe is stuff we can't see

explain 4 | Dark matter and dark energy
- Ordinary matter: about 5% of the universe's energy. Dark matter: about 27%. Dark energy: about 68%.
- Dark matter shows up in galaxy rotation and gravitational lensing.
- Dark energy drives the accelerating expansion, discovered in 1998 using supernovae.
> Physics 3 done. That's the whole physics track
